{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014H29rgwPWkKwXT6jWeNGVt","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Review Guideline\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReview an implementation plan against the current codebase and existing measurements.\n6\t\n7\tThe job is not to paraphrase the plan. The job is to find:\n8\t\n9\t- correctness holes\n10\t- missing invariants\n11\t- interface mismatches with the current code\n12\t- places where estimated wins are not supported by measurements\n13\t- places where the plan is overcomplicated relative to expected proof-time benefit\n14\t- possible simplifications\n15\t\n16\t## Core standard\n17\t\n18\tA good review answers four questions:\n19\t\n20\t1. Is the proposal correct against the code that exists today?\n21\t2. Is it specific enough to implement a prototype without inventing missing design later?\n22\t3. Is it likely to improve the measured bottleneck it claims to target?\n23\t4. Is the proposed complexity justified by expected wall-clock benefit?\n24\t\n25\t## Review method\n26\t\n27\t### 1. Read the plan for concrete claims\n28\t\n29\tExtract:\n30\t\n31\t- the claimed bottleneck\n32\t- the proposed mechanism\n33\t- what is said to be AIR-level vs trace-level\n34\t- what is said to be reused, cached, batched, or hoisted\n35\t- expected savings and what units they use\n36\t\n37\tDo not accept vague claims like “should help” or “future cleanup will handle this.”\n38\t\n39\t### 2. Check the current code path end to end\n40\t\n41\tFind the actual call chain in the current implementation:\n42\t\n43\t- caller\n44\t- helper\n45\t- FFI boundary\n46\t- CUDA/kernel side if relevant\n47\t- postprocess / D2H / synchronization points\n48\t\n49\tVerify that the plan matches the real architecture, not an assumed one.\n50\t\n51\t### 3. Check measurements before trusting priorities\n52\t\n53\tUse existing profiling or instrumentation first.\n54\t\n55\tFor each claimed win, ask:\n56\t\n57\t- is this work actually inside the measured bottleneck?\n58\t- is the cited span wall-clock or only a subspan?\n59\t- is the estimate per segment, per round, or per proof?\n60\t\n61\tReject priority arguments that contradict the data.\n62\t\n63\t### 4. Separate design blockers from cleanup\n64\t\n65\tA design blocker is something that makes the proposal:\n66\t\n67\t- incorrect\n68\t- unimplementable as written\n69\t- likely to fail at runtime\n70\t- likely to miss the intended performance target\n71\t\n72\tCleanup issues are:\n73\t\n74\t- wording imprecision\n75\t- stale checklist items\n76\t- missing explicit `Default` impl mention\n77\t- test/checklist polish\n78\t\n79\tBe strict about the difference.\n80\t\n81\t## What to verify every time\n82\t\n83\t### Correctness / architecture\n84\t\n85\t- Does the proposal preserve current invariants?\n86\t- Does it account for all inputs needed to reproduce current behavior?\n87\t- Does it match current buffer layout, indexing, launch geometry, and reduction shape?\n88\t- Does it accidentally rely on information only available on the CUDA side or only on the Rust side?\n89\t- Does it handle fallback paths, not just the new fast path?\n90\t\n91\t### Memory / buffers\n92\t\n93\t- Is capacity confused with logical length?\n94\t- Are reused buffers read back to host with the correct live length?\n95\t- Does the plan assume async frees happen immediately?\n96\t- Is the memory budget a real bound or just a heuristic?\n97\t- If sub-batching is proposed, is the scope of reuse actually large enough to matter?\n98\t\n99\t### Performance realism\n100\t\n101\t- Is the plan attacking the span that is actually large in Nsight/NVTX?\n102\t- Are claimed savings plausible relative to the cited measured span?\n103\t- Is the proposal removing work, or only moving it around?\n104\t- Is the proposal saving GPU time, CPU time, runtime API overhead, or sync time? Be explicit.\n105\t\n106\t### Implementation readiness\n107\t\n108\t- Are required new structs/helpers/FFI changes fully specified?\n109\t- Are raw pointers, lifetimes, or ownership changes spelled out?\n110\t- Does the checklist include all touched paths?\n111\t- Are there hidden “to be solved later” gaps in core behavior?\n112\t\n113\t## Preferred review output structure\n114\t\n115\tUse:\n116\t\n117\t1. `Findings`\n118\t2. `Improvements I would consider`\n119\t3. `Assessment`\n120\t\n121\tUnder `Findings`:\n122\t\n123\t- order by severity\n124\t- use precise statements\n125\t- include file references\n126\t- say exactly what is wrong and what must change\n127\t\n128\tUnder `Improvements I would consider`:\n129\t\n130\t- only include real alternatives that could better improve proof time\n131\t- distinguish these from blockers\n132\t\n133\tUnder `Assessment`:\n134\t\n135\t- state whether there is a remaining design-level blocker\n136\t- state whether the plan is prototype-ready\n137\t- say what the strongest part of the plan is\n138\t\n139\t## Writing rules\n140\t\n141\t- Be concrete, not rhetorical.\n142\t- Prefer “As written, X is wrong because Y in current code does Z.”\n143\t- Do not say “looks good overall” before findings.\n144\t- Do not invent performance numbers.\n145\t- Do not call something “overengineering” unless you explain what simpler path exists.\n146\t- Do not let “future work” hide a present correctness or implementation gap.\n147\t- If the proposal is good, say so plainly after checking it.\n148\t\n149\t## Useful review heuristics\n150\t\n151\t- Cached data must include every static input needed to rebuild current behavior.\n152\t- Reuse scoped inside a function call does not help if the costly repetition happens across calls.\n153\t- Exact-size host copies matter when buffer APIs do not track logical length separately.\n154\t- A plan that ignores fallback paths is usually incomplete.\n155\t- A measured `~100us` span is not a serious optimization target inside a `~10ms+` phase.\n156\t- The cleanest prototype path is usually the one that matches an existing pattern already used elsewhere in the codebase.\n157\t\n158\t## Minimum bar before approving a prototype plan\n159\t\n160\tApprove only if all of these are true:\n161\t\n162\t- no remaining design-level correctness blocker\n163\t- no missing data needed to reproduce current behavior\n164\t- no hidden interface gap between Rust and CUDA\n165\t- the intended win targets a measured bottleneck\n166\t- the checklist is sufficient for someone else to implement the prototype without guessing\n167\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012DnErZgkr5B8FMDxVSwvp7","session_id":"d96bd5ad-dc55-4cdf-ae15-7b7ce8dfed52","uuid":"537be76a-0d4e-4c6a-8e8e-8b61535fe522","timestamp":"2026-04-13T09:00:36.354Z"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01B3yPBT6VCzkqjaQC9d6APe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# GPU Prover Optimization Context\n2\t\n3\t## Setup\n4\t\n5\t### Repositories\n6\t\n7\t- stark-backend: https://github.com/powdr-labs/stark-backend, branch `v2-powdr-07-04`. Starting from this branch, start a new branch called `autoopt-<YYYY-MM-DD>` for your work.\n8\t- openvm: https://github.com/powdr-labs/openvm, branch `v2-powdr-07-04`\n9\t- powdr: https://github.com/powdr-labs/powdr, branch `openvm-v2-integration-07-04`\n10\t\n11\tThe powdr repo's Cargo.toml uses local patches for openvm and stark-backend, so all three repos must be checked out side-by-side.\n12\t\n13\t### Tools\n14\t\n15\tDownload `spec.py` for analyzing metrics files:\n16\thttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/powdr-labs/powdr/refs/heads/main/openvm/metrics-viewer/spec.py\n17\t\n18\tDownload these scripts from the powdr **main** branch (they are out-of-date on the working branch):\n19\thttps://github.com/powdr-labs/powdr/tree/main/openvm-riscv/scripts\n20\t\n21\tThis includes `basic_metrics.py` for creating combined metrics files.\n22\t\n23\t## Problem Description\n24\t\n25\tWe're analyzing OpenVM v2 with autoprecompiles (APCs). Going from 0 to 300 APCs, trace cells, bus interaction messages, and constraint instances all decrease by >2x. With the CPU prover, STARK time (excl. trace) scales accordingly. With the GPU prover, it does not.\n26\t\n27\tThe goal is to make the GPU prover's \"STARK excl trace\" metric scale with the reduction in cells, bus interaction messages, and constraint instances as APCs increase (>2x improvement from APC 0 to APC 300).\n28\t\n29\tFocus ONLY on \"STARK excl trace\" — do not optimize other proving phases like trace gen or preflight execution.\n30\t\n31\tFor more context, see [this writeup](https://gist.github.com/leonardoalt/cb8fba32b08669fc63dea4d7ab0b67af).\n32\tMake sure you read it in full and explore the linked resources (e.g. the paper).\n33\t\n34\t## Measuring Results\n35\t\n36\tBefore running any benchmark, check that the GPU is free by running `nvidia-smi`. If another process is using the GPU, wait for it to finish or kill it before proceeding. Benchmarks will fail with OOM if the GPU memory is partially occupied.\n37\t\n38\tIn the powdr repo, run:\n39\t\n40\t```bash\n41\topenvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tfor APCs {0, 100, 300}. Results go to `results/pairing/`, each config producing a `metrics.json`.\n45\t\n46\tThe script compiles the guest and generates autoprecompiles once (this is slow on the first run), then tests prover changes without recompilation.\n47\t\n48\t### Analysis Tool\n49\t\n50\tUse `spec.py` (downloaded in Setup) to analyze the metrics files:\n51\t```\n52\tpython spec.py <metrics_json_path> <experiment_name>\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\tThis gives a fine-granular breakdown of proof time. An example run:\n56\t\n57\t```\n58\t$ python spec.py https://gist.githubusercontent.com/leonardoalt/f2b810eaf2d5d37491da491f496639d1/raw/551f8590bd372dc909db3d3da2235b6a8aff70d0/metrics_pairing_v2_combined.json metrics_pairing_v2_apc300\n59\t\n60\tExperiment: metrics_pairing_v2_apc300  (OpenVM 2)\n61\t\n62\t  App Proof Basic Stats\n63\t  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n64\t  Segments                  2\n65\t  AIR Instances             623\n66\t  Columns                   106,842\n67\t  Cells                     811.18M (811,182,720)\n68\t  Constraints               54,961\n69\t  Constraint Instances      505.24M (505,240,712)\n70\t  Bus Interactions          67,357\n71\t  Bus Interaction Messages  448.46M (448,457,256)\n72\t\n73\t  Proof Time\n74\t  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n75\t  Metered Execution        0.67s (669 ms)  (  8.6%)\n76\t  App Proof Time           5.90s (5896 ms)  ( 76.1%)\n77\t    STARK (excl. trace)    2.58s (2579 ms)  ( 33.3%)\n78\t      Constraints          1.63s (1628 ms)  ( 21.0%)\n79\t        LogUp GKR          0.78s (781 ms)  ( 10.1%)\n80\t        Round 0            0.66s (662 ms)  (  8.5%)\n81\t        MLE Rounds         0.18s (182 ms)  (  2.3%)\n82\t        Other              0.00s (3 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n83\t      Openings             0.51s (514 ms)  (  6.6%)\n84\t        WHIR               0.19s (188 ms)  (  2.4%)\n85\t        Stacked Reduction  0.33s (325 ms)  (  4.2%)\n86\t        Other              0.00s (1 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n87\t      Trace Commit         0.43s (434 ms)  (  5.6%)\n88\t      Other                0.00s (3 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n89\t    Preflight Execution    1.83s (1830 ms)  ( 23.6%)\n90\t    Set Initial Memory     0.20s (203 ms)  (  2.6%)\n91\t    Trace Gen              1.28s (1282 ms)  ( 16.6%)\n92\t    Other                  0.00s (2 ms)  (  0.0%) (residual)\n93\t  Leaf Recursion           0.50s (502 ms)  (  6.5%)\n94\t  Inner Recursion          0.41s (409 ms)  (  5.3%)\n95\t  Compression              0.27s (270 ms)  (  3.5%)\n96\t  ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97\t  Total                    7.75s (7746 ms)  (100.0%)\n98\t```\n99\t\n100\tWe would expect \"STARK (excl. trace)\" to scale with a combination of cells, bus interaction messages and constraint instances, which are all going down by a factor of >2x when going from 0 to 300 APCs. This is not the case in practice on GPU though.\n101\t\n102\t## Profiling\n103\t\n104\tNVIDIA nsight and nvidia-smi are available. Use them to understand GPU performance characteristics and bottlenecks.\n105\t\n106\t## Constraints\n107\t\n108\t- Make sure you only do work in the `stark-backend` repository, and on the (newly created) `autoopt-<YYYY-MM-DD>` branch.\n109\t- Do NOT change the underlying cryptographic protocol.\n110\t- Do NOT change the verifier.\n111\t- Do NOT optimize other proving phases, aside from \"STARK (excl. trace)\"\n112\t\n113\t## Result Files\n114\t\n115\tFor each task, collect:\n116\t- `before_apc{000,100,300}.json` — metrics before the change\n117\t- `after_apc{000,100,300}.json` — metrics after the change\n118\t- `metrics_combined.json` — created using `basic_metrics.py`\n119\t- Relevant profiling files (nsight exports, etc.)\n120\t\n121\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012DnErZgkr5B8FMDxVSwvp7","session_id":"d96bd5ad-dc55-4cdf-ae15-7b7ce8dfed52","uuid":"3a36a1a6-cd66-451c-b4fa-3f820d8612c5","timestamp":"2026-04-13T09:00:36.354Z"}
