{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KwZv8xM48BqaeJNB7tbC4X","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch MLE Interpolation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace per-AIR `interpolate_columns_gpu` kernel launches in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch per round. This targets the MLE Rounds span under Constraints (`prover.rap_constraints.mle_rounds`), which is 180ms at APC 300 — completely unoptimized since baseline.\n6\t\n7\tNsys profiling shows 4993 `interpolate_columns_kernel` launches with only 20ms total GPU compute time. Each of the ~310 per-round launches incurs: (1) a `cudaMallocAsync` for the interpolated output buffer, (2) a `to_device()` H2D copy for column pointers, (3) a CUDA kernel launch. At ~15μs overhead per call × 4993 calls = ~75ms of pure overhead, which dominates the 180ms span.\n8\t\n9\t## Current Code Path\n10\t\n11\t### Per-round interpolation loop (the target)\n12\t\n13\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1128-1293\n14\t\n15\tIn `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()`, called once per MLE round (line 515), the function iterates over all traces. For each trace in Case B (round <= n_lift, line 1209):\n16\t\n17\t1. **Line 1216-1226**: Build `columns: Vec<*const EF>` — collects pointers to all columns (selectors + preprocessed + mains) from the current trace's `DeviceMatrix` buffers.\n18\t2. **Line 1227**: `DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(sp_deg * num_y, columns.len())` — allocates a new device buffer for the interpolated output. This calls `cudaMallocAsync`.\n19\t3. **Line 1228**: `columns.to_device()` — allocates a small `DeviceBuffer<*const EF>` and copies the pointer array H2D. This calls `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync`.\n20\t4. **Line 1229-1231**: `interpolate_columns_gpu(...)` — launches one `interpolate_columns_kernel` invocation.\n21\t5. **Line 1275**: `_keepalive_interpolated.push(interpolated)` — stores the interpolated buffer to keep it alive for downstream evaluation kernels.\n22\t6. **Line 1278-1292**: Builds `TraceCtx` with pointers into the interpolated buffer.\n23\t\n24\tAt APC 300 with ~310 AIRs per segment and ~8 rounds of interpolation on average, this produces ~2500 iterations per segment × 2 segments = ~5000 total kernel launches.\n25\t\n26\t### The CUDA kernel\n27\t\n28\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`, lines 71-92\n29\t\n30\t```cuda\n31\t__global__ void interpolate_columns_kernel(\n32\t    FpExt *interpolated, const FpExt *const *columns,\n33\t    uint32_t s_deg, uint32_t num_y, uint32_t num_columns\n34\t) {\n35\t    // Each thread handles one (col, y) pair\n36\t    // Reads t0=column[2*y], t1=column[2*y+1]\n37\t    // Writes s_deg interpolated values: t0 + (t1-t0) * Fp(x+1) for x in 0..s_deg\n38\t}\n39\t```\n40\t\n41\t**Launcher**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`, lines 190-201\n42\t\n43\t```c\n44\textern \"C\" int _interpolate_columns(\n45\t    FpExt *interpolated, const FpExt *const *columns,\n46\t    size_t s_deg, size_t num_y, size_t num_columns\n47\t) {\n48\t    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(num_y * num_columns, 512);\n49\t    interpolate_columns_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(interpolated, columns, s_deg, num_y, num_columns);\n50\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n51\t}\n52\t```\n53\t\n54\t**Rust wrapper**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, lines 518-531\n55\t\n56\t### Downstream consumers\n57\t\n58\tThe interpolated buffer is consumed via `TraceCtx` pointers by:\n59\t- `evaluate_logup_batched()` (line 1342-1353)\n60\t- `evaluate_zerocheck_batched()` (line 1375-1383)\n61\t- `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch` (line 1392-1406)\n62\t- `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` (line 1416-1423)\n63\t\n64\tThese evaluation functions only read from the interpolated data via device pointers stored in `TraceCtx.sels_ptr`, `TraceCtx.prep_ptr`, and `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev`. They do not depend on the interpolated data being in individual `DeviceMatrix` objects — only the raw device pointers matter.\n65\t\n66\t## Changes\n67\t\n68\t### Change 1: Add `InterpColDesc` struct to CUDA source\n69\t\n70\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n71\t\n72\tAdd after line 70 (before `interpolate_columns_kernel`):\n73\t\n74\t```cuda\n75\tstruct InterpColDesc {\n76\t    FpExt* output;              // Base pointer for this trace's interpolated output\n77\t    uint32_t columns_offset;    // Offset into the flattened columns array\n78\t    uint32_t num_y;             // Number of y-values for this trace\n79\t    uint32_t num_columns;       // Number of columns for this trace\n80\t    uint32_t total_threads;     // = num_y * num_columns (precomputed for efficiency)\n81\t};\n82\t```\n83\t\n84\t### Change 2: Add batched interpolation kernel to CUDA source\n85\t\n86\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n87\t\n88\tAdd after the existing `interpolate_columns_kernel`:\n89\t\n90\t```cuda\n91\t__global__ void batched_interpolate_columns_kernel(\n92\t    const InterpColDesc* descs,\n93\t    const FpExt *const * all_columns,\n94\t    uint32_t s_deg,\n95\t    uint32_t num_descs\n96\t) {\n97\t    uint32_t desc_idx = blockIdx.x;\n98\t    if (desc_idx >= num_descs) return;\n99\t\n100\t    const InterpColDesc& d = descs[desc_idx];\n101\t    for (uint32_t tidx = threadIdx.x; tidx < d.total_threads; tidx += blockDim.x) {\n102\t        uint32_t y = tidx % d.num_y;\n103\t        uint32_t col_local = tidx / d.num_y;\n104\t        if (col_local >= d.num_columns) continue;\n105\t\n106\t        const FpExt *column = all_columns[d.columns_offset + col_local];\n107\t        auto t0 = column[y << 1];\n108\t        auto t1 = column[(y << 1) | 1];\n109\t        FpExt *this_out = d.output + col_local * s_deg * d.num_y;\n110\t\n111\t        for (int x = 0; x < s_deg; x++) {\n112\t            this_out[x * d.num_y + y] = t0 + (t1 - t0) * Fp(x + 1u);\n113\t        }\n114\t    }\n115\t}\n116\t```\n117\t\n118\tDesign: one block per descriptor (= per trace). Each block's threads cooperatively process all (column, y) pairs for that trace, looping if `total_threads > blockDim.x`. This keeps the output layout identical to the existing kernel.\n119\t\n120\t### Change 3: Add batched launcher to CUDA source\n121\t\n122\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n123\t\n124\tAdd after the existing `_interpolate_columns` launcher:\n125\t\n126\t```c\n127\textern \"C\" int _batched_interpolate_columns(\n128\t    const InterpColDesc* descs,\n129\t    const FpExt *const * all_columns,\n130\t    size_t s_deg,\n131\t    size_t num_descs,\n132\t    size_t threads_per_block\n133\t) {\n134\t    if (num_descs == 0) return 0;\n135\t    dim3 grid(num_descs);\n136\t    dim3 block(threads_per_block);\n137\t    batched_interpolate_columns_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(\n138\t        descs, all_columns, s_deg, num_descs\n139\t    );\n140\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n141\t}\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\t### Change 4: Add `InterpColDesc` Rust struct and FFI bindings\n145\t\n146\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n147\t\n148\tAdd the Rust descriptor struct:\n149\t\n150\t```rust\n151\t#[repr(C)]\n152\t#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\n153\tpub struct InterpColDesc {\n154\t    pub output: *mut EF,\n155\t    pub columns_offset: u32,\n156\t    pub num_y: u32,\n157\t    pub num_columns: u32,\n158\t    pub total_threads: u32,\n159\t}\n160\tunsafe impl Send for InterpColDesc {}\n161\tunsafe impl Sync for InterpColDesc {}\n162\t```\n163\t\n164\tAdd the extern declaration alongside the existing `_interpolate_columns`:\n165\t\n166\t```rust\n167\tfn _batched_interpolate_columns(\n168\t    descs: *const InterpColDesc,\n169\t    all_columns: *const *const EF,\n170\t    s_deg: usize,\n171\t    num_descs: usize,\n172\t    threads_per_block: usize,\n173\t) -> i32;\n174\t```\n175\t\n176\tAdd a safe wrapper:\n177\t\n178\t```rust\n179\tpub unsafe fn batched_interpolate_columns_gpu(\n180\t    descs: &DeviceBuffer<InterpColDesc>,\n181\t    all_columns: &DeviceBuffer<*const EF>,\n182\t    s_deg: usize,\n183\t    threads_per_block: usize,\n184\t) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n185\t    CudaError::from_result(_batched_interpolate_columns(\n186\t        descs.as_ptr(),\n187\t        all_columns.as_ptr(),\n188\t        s_deg,\n189\t        descs.len(),\n190\t        threads_per_block,\n191\t    ))\n192\t}\n193\t```\n194\t\n195\t### Change 5: Restructure `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` to batch interpolation\n196\t\n197\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n198\t\n199\tReplace the per-trace interpolation loop (lines 1128-1293) with a two-phase approach:\n200\t\n201\t**Phase 1: Collect metadata and compute buffer sizes (CPU-only, no CUDA calls)**\n202\t\n203\tFirst pass over all traces, identical case-splitting logic as today. For each Case B trace:\n204\t- Compute `num_columns` (selectors width + all matrix widths)\n205\t- Compute `num_y = 1 << (n_lift - round)`\n206\t- Compute `interpolated_size = sp_deg * num_y * num_columns` (elements)\n207\t- Accumulate total buffer size across all Case B traces\n208\t- Record per-trace metadata: (trace_idx, air_idx, num_columns, num_y, interpolated_offset, column_start_idx)\n209\t- Collect all column pointers into a single flat `Vec<*const EF>`\n210\t\n211\tFor Case A traces, handle exactly as before (push to `late_eval`).\n212\t\n213\t**Phase 2: Batch allocate and launch (minimal CUDA calls)**\n214\t\n215\t1. Allocate one big `DeviceBuffer<EF>` of size = total interpolated elements across all traces\n216\t2. Build `Vec<InterpColDesc>`, one per Case B trace, with `output` pointing to the trace's offset within the big buffer\n217\t3. Upload descriptors and column pointers: `descs.to_device()`, `all_columns.to_device()`\n218\t4. Launch `batched_interpolate_columns_gpu` — one kernel call for all traces\n219\t\n220\t**Phase 3: Build TraceCtx (same as before)**\n221\t\n222\tFor each Case B trace, compute `sels_ptr`, `prep_ptr`, `main_ptrs_dev` as pointer offsets into the big buffer. The offsets are identical to what the current code computes (lines 1236-1271), just using the trace's offset within the big buffer rather than a per-trace `DeviceMatrix`.\n223\t\n224\tThe `_keepalive_interpolated` vector is replaced by keeping the single big `DeviceBuffer` alive for the duration of the evaluation phase.\n225\t\n226\t### Change 6: Choose block size for the batched kernel\n227\t\n228\tUse `threads_per_block = 256` (matching existing CUDA conventions in this codebase). For traces where `total_threads > 256`, the thread loop (`for tidx = threadIdx.x; tidx < total_threads; tidx += blockDim.x`) handles the excess. For traces where `total_threads < 256`, excess threads exit early via the `if (col_local >= num_columns) continue` guard.\n229\t\n230\t## Invariants\n231\t\n232\t1. **Output layout is identical**: Each trace's interpolated data has the same column-major layout as before: `interpolated[col * sp_deg * num_y + x * num_y + y]`. The `TraceCtx` pointers reference the same memory layout.\n233\t\n234\t2. **Downstream evaluation is unchanged**: The `evaluate_logup_batched`, `evaluate_zerocheck_batched`, monomial batch, and monomial par-y batch functions receive the same `TraceCtx` with the same device pointers. No changes needed to evaluation or fold paths.\n235\t\n236\t3. **Late traces (Case A) are unchanged**: The late_eval path (lines 1154-1207) does not call `interpolate_columns_gpu` and remains untouched.\n237\t\n238\t4. **Correctness of interpolation**: The batched kernel computes the same per-thread formula as the original: `t0 + (t1 - t0) * Fp(x + 1)`. Same indexing into column data, same output stride.\n239\t\n240\t5. **Memory lifetime**: The big interpolated buffer must remain live until all evaluation kernels complete. Storing it in a local variable that outlives the evaluation calls (same scope as the current `_keepalive_interpolated`) suffices.\n241\t\n242\t6. **s_deg is uniform**: All traces in a given round use the same `s_deg` (= `self.constraint_degree`), so it can be passed as a scalar to the batched kernel.\n243\t\n244\t## Measurement Plan\n245\t\n246\t### Before changes\n247\t\n248\tRun the benchmark for APC {0, 100, 300} and record `spec.py` output. Save as `before_apc{000,100,300}.json`.\n249\t\n250\t### After changes\n251\t\n252\tRun the same benchmark suite. Save as `after_apc{000,100,300}.json`.\n253\t\n254\t### Key metrics to compare\n255\t\n256\t1. **MLE Rounds** (`prover.rap_constraints.mle_rounds`): Primary target. Expect 180ms → ~120-130ms at APC 300 (30-40% improvement).\n257\t2. **STARK excl trace**: Expect 1466ms → ~1400-1410ms at APC 300 (4-5% improvement).\n258\t3. **APC 0 STARK excl trace**: Expect no regression (within noise of 2155ms). At APC 0, only ~99 AIRs, so the per-AIR overhead reduction is smaller.\n259\t4. **Correctness**: `prove+verify` must pass for all three configs.\n260\t\n261\t### Profiling\n262\t\n263\tRun nsys on APC 300 after the change. Verify:\n264\t- `interpolate_columns_kernel` launches drop from ~4993 to ~16 (one batched launch per round × 2 segments)\n265\t- Total `interpolate_columns` GPU time should remain ~20ms (same computation, different launch pattern)\n266\t- H2D copy count should decrease significantly\n267\t\n268\t## Rollback Criteria\n269\t\n270\tRevert if ANY of the following:\n271\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves less than 20ms (below noise floor)\n272\t- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 50ms\n273\t- `prove+verify` fails for any config\n274\t- GPU OOM on RTX 4090 (24GB)\n275\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves less than 15ms\n276\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d96bd5ad-dc55-4cdf-ae15-7b7ce8dfed52","uuid":"4fcf30ea-4e96-44b2-9c6d-8d4da9c6eac3","timestamp":"2026-04-13T09:04:46.130Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-1100-batch-mle-interpolation/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Batch MLE Interpolation\n\n## Goal\n\nReplace per-AIR `interpolate_columns_gpu` kernel launches in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch per round. This targets the MLE Rounds span under Constraints (`prover.rap_constraints.mle_rounds`), which is 180ms at APC 300 — completely unoptimized since baseline.\n\nNsys profiling shows 4993 `interpolate_columns_kernel` launches with only 20ms total GPU compute time. Each of the ~310 per-round launches incurs: (1) a `cudaMallocAsync` for the interpolated output buffer, (2) a `to_device()` H2D copy for column pointers, (3) a CUDA kernel launch. At ~15μs overhead per call × 4993 calls = ~75ms of pure overhead, which dominates the 180ms span.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Per-round interpolation loop (the target)\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1128-1293\n\nIn `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()`, called once per MLE round (line 515), the function iterates over all traces. For each trace in Case B (round <= n_lift, line 1209):\n\n1. **Line 1216-1226**: Build `columns: Vec<*const EF>` — collects pointers to all columns (selectors + preprocessed + mains) from the current trace's `DeviceMatrix` buffers.\n2. **Line 1227**: `DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(sp_deg * num_y, columns.len())` — allocates a new device buffer for the interpolated output. This calls `cudaMallocAsync`.\n3. **Line 1228**: `columns.to_device()` — allocates a small `DeviceBuffer<*const EF>` and copies the pointer array H2D. This calls `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync`.\n4. **Line 1229-1231**: `interpolate_columns_gpu(...)` — launches one `interpolate_columns_kernel` invocation.\n5. **Line 1275**: `_keepalive_interpolated.push(interpolated)` — stores the interpolated buffer to keep it alive for downstream evaluation kernels.\n6. **Line 1278-1292**: Builds `TraceCtx` with pointers into the interpolated buffer.\n\nAt APC 300 with ~310 AIRs per segment and ~8 rounds of interpolation on average, this produces ~2500 iterations per segment × 2 segments = ~5000 total kernel launches.\n\n### The CUDA kernel\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`, lines 71-92\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void interpolate_columns_kernel(\n    FpExt *interpolated, const FpExt *const *columns,\n    uint32_t s_deg, uint32_t num_y, uint32_t num_columns\n) {\n    // Each thread handles one (col, y) pair\n    // Reads t0=column[2*y], t1=column[2*y+1]\n    // Writes s_deg interpolated values: t0 + (t1-t0) * Fp(x+1) for x in 0..s_deg\n}\n```\n\n**Launcher**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`, lines 190-201\n\n```c\nextern \"C\" int _interpolate_columns(\n    FpExt *interpolated, const FpExt *const *columns,\n    size_t s_deg, size_t num_y, size_t num_columns\n) {\n    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(num_y * num_columns, 512);\n    interpolate_columns_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(interpolated, columns, s_deg, num_y, num_columns);\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n**Rust wrapper**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, lines 518-531\n\n### Downstream consumers\n\nThe interpolated buffer is consumed via `TraceCtx` pointers by:\n- `evaluate_logup_batched()` (line 1342-1353)\n- `evaluate_zerocheck_batched()` (line 1375-1383)\n- `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch` (line 1392-1406)\n- `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` (line 1416-1423)\n\nThese evaluation functions only read from the interpolated data via device pointers stored in `TraceCtx.sels_ptr`, `TraceCtx.prep_ptr`, and `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev`. They do not depend on the interpolated data being in individual `DeviceMatrix` objects — only the raw device pointers matter.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Add `InterpColDesc` struct to CUDA source\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n\nAdd after line 70 (before `interpolate_columns_kernel`):\n\n```cuda\nstruct InterpColDesc {\n    FpExt* output;              // Base pointer for this trace's interpolated output\n    uint32_t columns_offset;    // Offset into the flattened columns array\n    uint32_t num_y;             // Number of y-values for this trace\n    uint32_t num_columns;       // Number of columns for this trace\n    uint32_t total_threads;     // = num_y * num_columns (precomputed for efficiency)\n};\n```\n\n### Change 2: Add batched interpolation kernel to CUDA source\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n\nAdd after the existing `interpolate_columns_kernel`:\n\n```cuda\n__global__ void batched_interpolate_columns_kernel(\n    const InterpColDesc* descs,\n    const FpExt *const * all_columns,\n    uint32_t s_deg,\n    uint32_t num_descs\n) {\n    uint32_t desc_idx = blockIdx.x;\n    if (desc_idx >= num_descs) return;\n\n    const InterpColDesc& d = descs[desc_idx];\n    for (uint32_t tidx = threadIdx.x; tidx < d.total_threads; tidx += blockDim.x) {\n        uint32_t y = tidx % d.num_y;\n        uint32_t col_local = tidx / d.num_y;\n        if (col_local >= d.num_columns) continue;\n\n        const FpExt *column = all_columns[d.columns_offset + col_local];\n        auto t0 = column[y << 1];\n        auto t1 = column[(y << 1) | 1];\n        FpExt *this_out = d.output + col_local * s_deg * d.num_y;\n\n        for (int x = 0; x < s_deg; x++) {\n            this_out[x * d.num_y + y] = t0 + (t1 - t0) * Fp(x + 1u);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nDesign: one block per descriptor (= per trace). Each block's threads cooperatively process all (column, y) pairs for that trace, looping if `total_threads > blockDim.x`. This keeps the output layout identical to the existing kernel.\n\n### Change 3: Add batched launcher to CUDA source\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n\nAdd after the existing `_interpolate_columns` launcher:\n\n```c\nextern \"C\" int _batched_interpolate_columns(\n    const InterpColDesc* descs,\n    const FpExt *const * all_columns,\n    size_t s_deg,\n    size_t num_descs,\n    size_t threads_per_block\n) {\n    if (num_descs == 0) return 0;\n    dim3 grid(num_descs);\n    dim3 block(threads_per_block);\n    batched_interpolate_columns_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(\n        descs, all_columns, s_deg, num_descs\n    );\n    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n}\n```\n\n### Change 4: Add `InterpColDesc` Rust struct and FFI bindings\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n\nAdd the Rust descriptor struct:\n\n```rust\n#[repr(C)]\n#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\npub struct InterpColDesc {\n    pub output: *mut EF,\n    pub columns_offset: u32,\n    pub num_y: u32,\n    pub num_columns: u32,\n    pub total_threads: u32,\n}\nunsafe impl Send for InterpColDesc {}\nunsafe impl Sync for InterpColDesc {}\n```\n\nAdd the extern declaration alongside the existing `_interpolate_columns`:\n\n```rust\nfn _batched_interpolate_columns(\n    descs: *const InterpColDesc,\n    all_columns: *const *const EF,\n    s_deg: usize,\n    num_descs: usize,\n    threads_per_block: usize,\n) -> i32;\n```\n\nAdd a safe wrapper:\n\n```rust\npub unsafe fn batched_interpolate_columns_gpu(\n    descs: &DeviceBuffer<InterpColDesc>,\n    all_columns: &DeviceBuffer<*const EF>,\n    s_deg: usize,\n    threads_per_block: usize,\n) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n    CudaError::from_result(_batched_interpolate_columns(\n        descs.as_ptr(),\n        all_columns.as_ptr(),\n        s_deg,\n        descs.len(),\n        threads_per_block,\n    ))\n}\n```\n\n### Change 5: Restructure `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` to batch interpolation\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\nReplace the per-trace interpolation loop (lines 1128-1293) with a two-phase approach:\n\n**Phase 1: Collect metadata and compute buffer sizes (CPU-only, no CUDA calls)**\n\nFirst pass over all traces, identical case-splitting logic as today. For each Case B trace:\n- Compute `num_columns` (selectors width + all matrix widths)\n- Compute `num_y = 1 << (n_lift - round)`\n- Compute `interpolated_size = sp_deg * num_y * num_columns` (elements)\n- Accumulate total buffer size across all Case B traces\n- Record per-trace metadata: (trace_idx, air_idx, num_columns, num_y, interpolated_offset, column_start_idx)\n- Collect all column pointers into a single flat `Vec<*const EF>`\n\nFor Case A traces, handle exactly as before (push to `late_eval`).\n\n**Phase 2: Batch allocate and launch (minimal CUDA calls)**\n\n1. Allocate one big `DeviceBuffer<EF>` of size = total interpolated elements across all traces\n2. Build `Vec<InterpColDesc>`, one per Case B trace, with `output` pointing to the trace's offset within the big buffer\n3. Upload descriptors and column pointers: `descs.to_device()`, `all_columns.to_device()`\n4. Launch `batched_interpolate_columns_gpu` — one kernel call for all traces\n\n**Phase 3: Build TraceCtx (same as before)**\n\nFor each Case B trace, compute `sels_ptr`, `prep_ptr`, `main_ptrs_dev` as pointer offsets into the big buffer. The offsets are identical to what the current code computes (lines 1236-1271), just using the trace's offset within the big buffer rather than a per-trace `DeviceMatrix`.\n\nThe `_keepalive_interpolated` vector is replaced by keeping the single big `DeviceBuffer` alive for the duration of the evaluation phase.\n\n### Change 6: Choose block size for the batched kernel\n\nUse `threads_per_block = 256` (matching existing CUDA conventions in this codebase). For traces where `total_threads > 256`, the thread loop (`for tidx = threadIdx.x; tidx < total_threads; tidx += blockDim.x`) handles the excess. For traces where `total_threads < 256`, excess threads exit early via the `if (col_local >= num_columns) continue` guard.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Output layout is identical**: Each trace's interpolated data has the same column-major layout as before: `interpolated[col * sp_deg * num_y + x * num_y + y]`. The `TraceCtx` pointers reference the same memory layout.\n\n2. **Downstream evaluation is unchanged**: The `evaluate_logup_batched`, `evaluate_zerocheck_batched`, monomial batch, and monomial par-y batch functions receive the same `TraceCtx` with the same device pointers. No changes needed to evaluation or fold paths.\n\n3. **Late traces (Case A) are unchanged**: The late_eval path (lines 1154-1207) does not call `interpolate_columns_gpu` and remains untouched.\n\n4. **Correctness of interpolation**: The batched kernel computes the same per-thread formula as the original: `t0 + (t1 - t0) * Fp(x + 1)`. Same indexing into column data, same output stride.\n\n5. **Memory lifetime**: The big interpolated buffer must remain live until all evaluation kernels complete. Storing it in a local variable that outlives the evaluation calls (same scope as the current `_keepalive_interpolated`) suffices.\n\n6. **s_deg is uniform**: All traces in a given round use the same `s_deg` (= `self.constraint_degree`), so it can be passed as a scalar to the batched kernel.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\n### Before changes\n\nRun the benchmark for APC {0, 100, 300} and record `spec.py` output. Save as `before_apc{000,100,300}.json`.\n\n### After changes\n\nRun the same benchmark suite. Save as `after_apc{000,100,300}.json`.\n\n### Key metrics to compare\n\n1. **MLE Rounds** (`prover.rap_constraints.mle_rounds`): Primary target. Expect 180ms → ~120-130ms at APC 300 (30-40% improvement).\n2. **STARK excl trace**: Expect 1466ms → ~1400-1410ms at APC 300 (4-5% improvement).\n3. **APC 0 STARK excl trace**: Expect no regression (within noise of 2155ms). At APC 0, only ~99 AIRs, so the per-AIR overhead reduction is smaller.\n4. **Correctness**: `prove+verify` must pass for all three configs.\n\n### Profiling\n\nRun nsys on APC 300 after the change. Verify:\n- `interpolate_columns_kernel` launches drop from ~4993 to ~16 (one batched launch per round × 2 segments)\n- Total `interpolate_columns` GPU time should remain ~20ms (same computation, different launch pattern)\n- H2D copy count should decrease significantly\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of the following:\n- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves less than 20ms (below noise floor)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 50ms\n- `prove+verify` fails for any config\n- GPU OOM on RTX 4090 (24GB)\n- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves less than 15ms\n","numLines":276,"startLine":1,"totalLines":276}}}
