{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BvAVs5j9R5vRcXbb5GBuHg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch MLE Interpolation\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tReplace per-AIR `interpolate_columns_gpu` kernel launches in MLE sumcheck rounds with a single batched descriptor-array kernel launch per round. This targets the MLE Rounds span under **Constraints** (`prover.rap_constraints.mle_rounds`), which is 180ms at APC 300 — completely unoptimized since baseline. Note: this is a DIFFERENT metric from the Stacked Reduction MLE rounds under Openings (`prover.openings.stacked_reduction.mle_rounds`) that was optimized in task `2026-04-12-2330` and task `2026-04-13-2300`. The Constraints MLE Rounds has never been targeted.\n6\t\n7\tNsys profiling shows 4993 `interpolate_columns_kernel` launches with only 20ms total GPU compute time. Each of the ~310 per-round launches incurs: (1) a `cudaMallocAsync` for the interpolated output buffer, (2) a `to_device()` H2D copy for column pointers, (3) a CUDA kernel launch. At ~15μs overhead per call × 4993 calls = ~75ms of pure overhead, which dominates the 180ms span.\n8\t\n9\tA secondary source of overhead is per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` calls (~5000 `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync`), adding ~10-15ms. These are NOT batched by this plan because `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev` is a `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` consumed both as `.as_ptr()` in batch builders and as `&DeviceBuffer` in non-batch fallback paths (`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu`, `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu`). Changing the `TraceCtx` type would cascade through all evaluator code. This residual overhead is noted as follow-up work.\n10\t\n11\t## Current Code Path\n12\t\n13\t### Per-round interpolation loop (the target)\n14\t\n15\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`, lines 1128-1293\n16\t\n17\tIn `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()`, called once per MLE round (line 515), the function iterates over all traces. For each trace in Case B (round <= n_lift, line 1209):\n18\t\n19\t1. **Line 1216-1226**: Build `columns: Vec<*const EF>` — collects pointers to all columns (selectors + preprocessed + mains) from the current trace's `DeviceMatrix` buffers.\n20\t2. **Line 1227**: `DeviceMatrix::<EF>::with_capacity(sp_deg * num_y, columns.len())` — allocates a new device buffer for the interpolated output. This calls `cudaMallocAsync`.\n21\t3. **Line 1228**: `columns.to_device()` — allocates a small `DeviceBuffer<*const EF>` and copies the pointer array H2D. This calls `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync`.\n22\t4. **Line 1229-1231**: `interpolate_columns_gpu(...)` — launches one `interpolate_columns_kernel` invocation.\n23\t5. **Line 1275**: `_keepalive_interpolated.push(interpolated)` — stores the interpolated buffer to keep it alive for downstream evaluation kernels.\n24\t6. **Line 1278-1292**: Builds `TraceCtx` with pointers into the interpolated buffer.\n25\t\n26\tAt APC 300 with ~310 AIRs per segment and ~8 rounds of interpolation on average, this produces ~2500 iterations per segment × 2 segments = ~5000 total kernel launches.\n27\t\n28\t### The CUDA kernel\n29\t\n30\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`, lines 71-92\n31\t\n32\t```cuda\n33\t__global__ void interpolate_columns_kernel(\n34\t    FpExt *interpolated, const FpExt *const *columns,\n35\t    uint32_t s_deg, uint32_t num_y, uint32_t num_columns\n36\t) {\n37\t    // Each thread handles one (col, y) pair\n38\t    // Reads t0=column[2*y], t1=column[2*y+1]\n39\t    // Writes s_deg interpolated values: t0 + (t1-t0) * Fp(x+1) for x in 0..s_deg\n40\t}\n41\t```\n42\t\n43\t**Launcher**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`, lines 190-201\n44\t\n45\t```c\n46\textern \"C\" int _interpolate_columns(\n47\t    FpExt *interpolated, const FpExt *const *columns,\n48\t    size_t s_deg, size_t num_y, size_t num_columns\n49\t) {\n50\t    auto [grid, block] = kernel_launch_params(num_y * num_columns, 512);\n51\t    interpolate_columns_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(interpolated, columns, s_deg, num_y, num_columns);\n52\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n53\t}\n54\t```\n55\t\n56\t**Rust wrapper**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`, lines 518-531\n57\t\n58\t### Downstream consumers\n59\t\n60\tThe interpolated buffer is consumed via `TraceCtx` pointers by:\n61\t- `evaluate_logup_batched()` (line 1342-1353)\n62\t- `evaluate_zerocheck_batched()` (line 1375-1383)\n63\t- `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch` (line 1392-1406)\n64\t- `ZerocheckMonomialBatch` (line 1416-1423)\n65\t\n66\tThese evaluation functions only read from the interpolated data via device pointers stored in `TraceCtx.sels_ptr`, `TraceCtx.prep_ptr`, and `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev`. They do not depend on the interpolated data being in individual `DeviceMatrix` objects — only the raw device pointers matter.\n67\t\n68\t## Changes\n69\t\n70\t### Change 1: Add `InterpColDesc` struct to CUDA source\n71\t\n72\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n73\t\n74\tAdd after line 70 (before `interpolate_columns_kernel`):\n75\t\n76\t```cuda\n77\tstruct InterpColDesc {\n78\t    FpExt* output;              // Base pointer for this trace's interpolated output\n79\t    uint32_t columns_offset;    // Offset into the flattened columns array\n80\t    uint32_t num_y;             // Number of y-values for this trace\n81\t    uint32_t num_columns;       // Number of columns for this trace\n82\t    uint32_t total_threads;     // = num_y * num_columns (precomputed for efficiency)\n83\t};\n84\t```\n85\t\n86\t### Change 2: Add batched interpolation kernel to CUDA source\n87\t\n88\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n89\t\n90\tAdd after the existing `interpolate_columns_kernel`:\n91\t\n92\t```cuda\n93\t__global__ void batched_interpolate_columns_kernel(\n94\t    const InterpColDesc* descs,\n95\t    const FpExt *const * all_columns,\n96\t    uint32_t s_deg,\n97\t    uint32_t num_descs\n98\t) {\n99\t    uint32_t desc_idx = blockIdx.x;\n100\t    if (desc_idx >= num_descs) return;\n101\t\n102\t    const InterpColDesc& d = descs[desc_idx];\n103\t    for (uint32_t tidx = threadIdx.x; tidx < d.total_threads; tidx += blockDim.x) {\n104\t        uint32_t y = tidx % d.num_y;\n105\t        uint32_t col_local = tidx / d.num_y;\n106\t        if (col_local >= d.num_columns) continue;\n107\t\n108\t        const FpExt *column = all_columns[d.columns_offset + col_local];\n109\t        auto t0 = column[y << 1];\n110\t        auto t1 = column[(y << 1) | 1];\n111\t        FpExt *this_out = d.output + col_local * s_deg * d.num_y;\n112\t\n113\t        for (int x = 0; x < s_deg; x++) {\n114\t            this_out[x * d.num_y + y] = t0 + (t1 - t0) * Fp(x + 1u);\n115\t        }\n116\t    }\n117\t}\n118\t```\n119\t\n120\tDesign: one block per descriptor (= per trace). Each block's threads cooperatively process all (column, y) pairs for that trace, looping if `total_threads > blockDim.x`. This keeps the output layout identical to the existing kernel.\n121\t\n122\t### Change 3: Add batched launcher to CUDA source\n123\t\n124\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/utils.cu`\n125\t\n126\tAdd after the existing `_interpolate_columns` launcher:\n127\t\n128\t```c\n129\textern \"C\" int _batched_interpolate_columns(\n130\t    const InterpColDesc* descs,\n131\t    const FpExt *const * all_columns,\n132\t    size_t s_deg,\n133\t    size_t num_descs,\n134\t    size_t threads_per_block\n135\t) {\n136\t    if (num_descs == 0) return 0;\n137\t    dim3 grid(num_descs);\n138\t    dim3 block(threads_per_block);\n139\t    batched_interpolate_columns_kernel<<<grid, block>>>(\n140\t        descs, all_columns, s_deg, num_descs\n141\t    );\n142\t    return CHECK_KERNEL();\n143\t}\n144\t```\n145\t\n146\t### Change 4: Add `InterpColDesc` Rust struct and FFI bindings\n147\t\n148\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/cuda/logup_zerocheck.rs`\n149\t\n150\tAdd the Rust descriptor struct:\n151\t\n152\t```rust\n153\t#[repr(C)]\n154\t#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\n155\tpub struct InterpColDesc {\n156\t    pub output: *mut EF,\n157\t    pub columns_offset: u32,\n158\t    pub num_y: u32,\n159\t    pub num_columns: u32,\n160\t    pub total_threads: u32,\n161\t}\n162\tunsafe impl Send for InterpColDesc {}\n163\tunsafe impl Sync for InterpColDesc {}\n164\t```\n165\t\n166\tAdd the extern declaration alongside the existing `_interpolate_columns`:\n167\t\n168\t```rust\n169\tfn _batched_interpolate_columns(\n170\t    descs: *const InterpColDesc,\n171\t    all_columns: *const *const EF,\n172\t    s_deg: usize,\n173\t    num_descs: usize,\n174\t    threads_per_block: usize,\n175\t) -> i32;\n176\t```\n177\t\n178\tAdd a safe wrapper:\n179\t\n180\t```rust\n181\tpub unsafe fn batched_interpolate_columns_gpu(\n182\t    descs: &DeviceBuffer<InterpColDesc>,\n183\t    all_columns: &DeviceBuffer<*const EF>,\n184\t    s_deg: usize,\n185\t    threads_per_block: usize,\n186\t) -> Result<(), CudaError> {\n187\t    CudaError::from_result(_batched_interpolate_columns(\n188\t        descs.as_ptr(),\n189\t        all_columns.as_ptr(),\n190\t        s_deg,\n191\t        descs.len(),\n192\t        threads_per_block,\n193\t    ))\n194\t}\n195\t```\n196\t\n197\t### Change 5: Restructure `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` to batch interpolation\n198\t\n199\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n200\t\n201\tReplace the per-trace interpolation loop (lines 1128-1293) with a three-phase approach:\n202\t\n203\t**Phase 1: Collect metadata and compute buffer sizes (CPU-only, no CUDA calls)**\n204\t\n205\tFirst pass over all traces, identical case-splitting logic as today. For each Case B trace:\n206\t- Compute `num_columns` (selectors width + all matrix widths)\n207\t- Compute `num_y = 1 << (n_lift - round)`\n208\t- Compute `interpolated_size = sp_deg * num_y * num_columns` (elements)\n209\t- Accumulate total buffer size across all Case B traces\n210\t- Record per-trace metadata: (trace_idx, air_idx, num_columns, num_y, interpolated_offset, column_start_idx, has_preprocessed, need_rot, main matrix widths)\n211\t- Collect all column pointers into a single flat `Vec<*const EF>`\n212\t\n213\tFor Case A traces, handle exactly as before (push to `late_eval`).\n214\t\n215\t**Phase 2: Batch allocate and launch (minimal CUDA calls)**\n216\t\n217\t1. Allocate one big `DeviceBuffer<EF>` of size = total interpolated elements across all traces\n218\t2. Build `Vec<InterpColDesc>`, one per Case B trace, with `output` pointing to the trace's offset within the big buffer\n219\t3. Upload descriptors and column pointers: `descs.to_device()`, `all_columns.to_device()` — 2 H2D copies total\n220\t4. Launch `batched_interpolate_columns_gpu` — one kernel call for all traces\n221\t\n222\tThis replaces ~310 × (cudaMallocAsync + columns.to_device + kernel launch) with 1 × (cudaMallocAsync + 2 × to_device + kernel launch).\n223\t\n224\t**Phase 3: Build TraceCtx (per-trace main_ptrs.to_device still needed)**\n225\t\n226\tFor each Case B trace, compute `sels_ptr`, `prep_ptr` as pointer offsets into the big interpolated buffer. The `main_ptrs_dev` per-trace `to_device()` call remains because `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev` is `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` consumed both as `.as_ptr()` in batch builders and as `&DeviceBuffer` in fallback evaluator functions. Changing this would require modifying `TraceCtx` and all downstream evaluator code — deferred to follow-up.\n227\t\n228\tThe `_keepalive_interpolated` vector is replaced by keeping the single big `DeviceBuffer` alive for the duration of the evaluation phase.\n229\t\n230\t### Change 6: Choose block size for the batched kernel\n231\t\n232\tUse `threads_per_block = 256` (matching existing CUDA conventions in this codebase). For traces where `total_threads > 256`, the thread loop (`for tidx = threadIdx.x; tidx < total_threads; tidx += blockDim.x`) handles the excess. For traces where `total_threads < 256`, excess threads exit early via the `if (col_local >= num_columns) continue` guard.\n233\t\n234\t**Load imbalance note**: One-block-per-trace creates load imbalance because small APCs have `total_threads` as low as 1, while large AIRs may have 50K+. A flat-thread-grid with binary search would give better GPU utilization but adds implementation complexity (prefix-sum array upload, per-thread descriptor lookup). Since the optimization targets **launch overhead** (not GPU compute time), and the kernel's total GPU compute is only 20ms, the load imbalance in compute is acceptable. The alternative flat-grid design can be explored as follow-up if GPU compute becomes the bottleneck after launch overhead is eliminated.\n235\t\n236\t## Invariants\n237\t\n238\t1. **Output layout is identical**: Each trace's interpolated data has the same column-major layout as before: `interpolated[col * sp_deg * num_y + x * num_y + y]`. The `TraceCtx` pointers reference the same memory layout.\n239\t\n240\t2. **Downstream evaluation is unchanged**: The `evaluate_logup_batched`, `evaluate_zerocheck_batched`, monomial batch, and monomial par-y batch functions receive the same `TraceCtx` with the same device pointers. No changes needed to evaluation or fold paths.\n241\t\n242\t3. **Late traces (Case A) are unchanged**: The late_eval path (lines 1154-1207) does not call `interpolate_columns_gpu` and remains untouched.\n243\t\n244\t4. **Correctness of interpolation**: The batched kernel computes the same per-thread formula as the original: `t0 + (t1 - t0) * Fp(x + 1)`. Same indexing into column data, same output stride.\n245\t\n246\t5. **Memory lifetime**: The big interpolated buffer must remain live until all evaluation kernels complete. Storing it in a local variable that outlives the evaluation calls (same scope as the current `_keepalive_interpolated`) suffices.\n247\t\n248\t6. **s_deg is uniform**: All traces in a given round use the same `s_deg` (= `self.constraint_degree`), so it can be passed as a scalar to the batched kernel.\n249\t\n250\t## Measurement Plan\n251\t\n252\t### Before changes\n253\t\n254\tRun the benchmark for APC {0, 100, 300} and record `spec.py` output. Save as `before_apc{000,100,300}.json`.\n255\t\n256\t### After changes\n257\t\n258\tRun the same benchmark suite. Save as `after_apc{000,100,300}.json`.\n259\t\n260\t### Key metrics to compare\n261\t\n262\t1. **MLE Rounds** (`prover.rap_constraints.mle_rounds`): Primary target. Expect 180ms → ~130-150ms at APC 300 (17-28% improvement). This eliminates ~5000 interpolation kernel launches + ~5000 cudaMallocAsync for interpolation buffers + ~5000 columns.to_device() calls, but ~5000 main_ptrs.to_device() calls remain as residual overhead.\n263\t2. **STARK excl trace**: Expect 1466ms → ~1420-1440ms at APC 300 (2-3% improvement).\n264\t3. **APC 0 STARK excl trace**: Expect no regression (within noise of 2155ms). At APC 0, only ~99 AIRs, so the per-AIR overhead reduction is smaller.\n265\t4. **Correctness**: `prove+verify` must pass for all three configs.\n266\t\n267\t### Profiling\n268\t\n269\tRun nsys on APC 300 after the change. Verify:\n270\t- `interpolate_columns_kernel` launches drop from ~4993 to ~16 (one batched launch per round × 2 segments)\n271\t- Total `interpolate_columns` GPU time should remain ~20ms (same computation, different launch pattern)\n272\t- H2D copy count should decrease significantly\n273\t\n274\t## Rollback Criteria\n275\t\n276\tRevert if ANY of the following:\n277\t- MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves less than 20ms (below noise floor)\n278\t- STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by more than 50ms\n279\t- `prove+verify` fails for any config\n280\t- GPU OOM on RTX 4090 (24GB)\n281\t- STARK excl trace at APC 300 improves less than 15ms\n282\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01S4PXjgjCunJyXtNfJzVVX9","session_id":"d96bd5ad-dc55-4cdf-ae15-7b7ce8dfed52","uuid":"cf265606-ea78-4dd7-8998-1ca57f780b7b","timestamp":"2026-04-13T09:07:15.115Z"}
